  "query_preview": "..\nThe remnants of a prior cold front extend southwest into the region \nfrom beyond 25N160E to just east of the northern CNMI, where they \ninteract with an approaching surface trough. This is leading to \nscattered showers and isolated thunderstorms across Tinian and Saipan\nwaters. Models show this pattern will hold into at least Saturday, \nas the surface trough gradually crosses the Marianas and the weak \nconvergence boundary fragments and shifts west, maintaining most of \nthe showers and thunderstorms near Saipan and Tinian the next few \ndays. For Guam, scattered island-effect showers and isolated \nthunderstorms remain in the forecast for the afternoon hours of \nFriday and Saturday as winds are expected to be light to gentle out \nof the north to northeast on Friday, and light and variable Saturday.\nThe likelihood and strength of island-effect showers will depend on \nnearby convection and daytime cloud cover, which would diminish \ndiurnal island heating. Earlier this afternoon, island showers \ndeveloped just off the eastern shores of Guam and spread over \nsouthern portions of the island, but were much diminished compared to\nyesterday due to lingering cloud cover in the mid- and upper levels \nof the atmosphere. Early next week, the monsoon trough which extends \nacross much of Micronesia looks to flex slightly north toward the \nMarianas, which could bring better shower chances for Guam at times, \nmainly around Monday and Tuesday.",
